⭐ 1)  Final keyword usage 

==> If you mark a class as final, no other class can derive from it.

example :

class Base final {
public:
    void show() {}
};

// ❌ Error: Cannot inherit from a final class
class Derived : public Base { };

==> When used on a virtual method, final means derived classes cannot override this function.

class Base {
public:
    virtual void foo() final;  // cannot be overridden
}; 

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    // ❌ Error: foo is final in Base
    void foo() override { }
};

===========================================
Common interview pitfalls
===========================================
❌ Wrong: using final on non-virtual functions
class A {
    void foo() final;   // ❌ compile error: foo is not virtual
};

❌ Wrong: trying to override a final method
void foo() final;      // in base class

// later…
void foo() override { }  // ❌ error

❌ Wrong: trying to inherit from a final class
class A final { };

class B : public A { }; // ❌ error


⭐ 2)  default keyword usage

Q1: Why use default if compiler already generates functions automatically?

Answer:
Because the moment you define any constructor, the compiler stops generating others.
default restores them.

==>  Real-world usage examples
⭐ Make class non-copyable but movable

class A {
public:
    A(const A&) = delete;
    A& operator=(const A&) = delete;

    A(A&&) = default;
    A& operator=(A&&) = default;
};


==> Forgetting that defining one constructor deletes other constructiors 

class A {
public:
    A(int x) {}        // You defined a constructor
};

A a;   // ❌ Error: default constructor not generated


Fix:

A() = default;

==> Common Interview Pitfalls

❌ Defaulting a function that is impossible to generate
class A {
    std::unique_ptr<int> p;

public:
    A(const A&) = default;   // ❌ Error: unique_ptr is non-copyable
};

❌ Combining delete and default incorrectly
class A {
public:
    A() = delete;
    A() = default;   // ❌ You cannot re-enable after delete
};


⭐ 3)  lambda  alternative in c 

“C does not support lambdas. The closest alternatives are using function pointers, simulating closures with a struct containing state and a function pointer, or using GCC nested functions as a non-standard extension.”

✅ 1. Function Pointers (Most Common Alternative)

You create a normal function and pass its pointer.

Example:
#include <stdio.h>

void myFunc(int x) {
    printf("Value = %d\n", x);
}

void caller(void (*f)(int)) {
    f(10);
}

int main() {
    caller(myFunc);
    return 0;
}


✅ 2. Function Pointers + Context (Struct Closure Pattern)

Since lambdas in other languages capture variables,
in C we simulate this with a struct.

Example (Closure-like behavior):
#include <stdio.h>

struct Closure {
    int a;
    void (*func)(int, struct Closure*);
};

void addFunc(int x, struct Closure* c) {
    printf("%d\n", x + c->a);
}

int main() {
    struct Closure c = { .a = 5, .func = addFunc };

    c.func(10, &c);  // prints 15
}


This is how “closures” are usually emulated in C.

⭐  4)  how we can call abstract class members ?
==> “You can give a pure virtual function a definition, but you still cannot call it without a derived class object, because the class remains abstract and cannot be instantiated.”

You call non-pure methods using a derived class object.

Pure virtual methods are called through overridden methods in the derived class.

Pure virtual methods can also have a definition and be called using scope resolution.

==> ✅ Smallest possible example WITHOUT calling from derived class

This is the closest valid example:
We define a pure virtual function, but we never call it, because calling it requires an object.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base {
public:
    virtual void show() = 0;   // pure virtual
};

// pure virtual CAN have a body
void Base::show() {
    cout << "Pure virtual function body in Base\n";
}

int main() {
    // Base b;  // ❌ ERROR: cannot create instance of abstract class
}


✔ This compiles
✔ It shows a pure virtual function with a definition
✘ But you cannot call it unless a derived class exists.

⚠️ Why can't we call it like this?
❌ Wrong: Base::show();   // ERROR — member function requires an object


Pure virtual functions are non-static, so they require an instance.

But: Base is abstract → cannot create object
     Therefore → cannot call the function

⭐ 5) can we create insatnce of abstarct class?

No, you cannot create an abstract class object.

You can create pointers/references to an abstract class.

You can create a derived class instance and refer to it using the abstract base class pointer.

⭐ 6) difference between vectors and array ?
Array → Fixed size, stored in stack (usually), no dynamic resizing.
Vector → Dynamic size, stored in heap, automatically grows and offers many utility functions.

